The most common reason a custom style draws nothing while the provider’s pre-built style works is that the **`source-layer` names you’re using don’t match the layer names that actually exist in the vector tiles**.

### 1. Make sure the source is wired correctly  

**Prefer the TileJSON `url`** that the provider publishes (it contains the tile template, min/max zoom, bounds, and the list of source-layers).

```js
map.addSource('provider-basemap', {
  type: 'vector',
  url: 'https://tiles.example.com/tileset.json'   // <-- TileJSON endpoint
});
```

If you only have a raw `{z}/{x}/{y}` template, use `tiles` and add the missing metadata (`minzoom`, `maxzoom`, etc.), but the TileJSON route is usually available from hosted providers.

### 2. Find the exact source-layer names  

Open the TileJSON (just fetch the URL in a browser or `curl`). Look for the `vector_layers` array; each entry has a `id` field – that is the exact name you must use in every style layer that reads from this source.

```json
{
  "vector_layers": [
    { "id": "transportation", "minzoom": 5, "maxzoom": 14, ... },
    { "id": "water",          "minzoom": 0, "maxzoom": 14, ... },
    { "id": "landuse",        "minzoom": 0, "maxzoom": 14, ... }
    // …
  ]
}
```

### 3. Use those names in your style layers  

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'roads',
  type: 'line',
  source: 'provider-basemap',
  'source-layer': 'transportation',   // must match TileJSON exactly
  paint: { 'line-color': '#ff6600', 'line-width': 2 }
});

map.addLayer({
  id: 'water',
  type: 'fill',
  source: 'provider-basemap',
  'source-layer': 'water',
  paint: { 'fill-color': '#a0c8f0' }
});
```

A typo, a different case, or using a layer name from another schema (e.g., OpenMapTiles vs. Shortbread) will silently produce no output—no console error, just an empty map.

### 4. Insert your layers **below** the label symbols  

If you add layers after the style has already loaded, `map.addLayer()` without a second argument puts them on top of everything, which can hide the provider’s labels. Insert them before the first symbol layer:

```js
// Find the first symbol layer (usually a label)
const firstSymbol = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol').id;

// Add your custom layer beneath it
map.addLayer(customLayerObject, firstSymbol);
```

### 5. Quick checklist  

| What to verify | How |
|----------------|-----|
| Source definition uses `url` (TileJSON) or a complete `tiles` array with `minzoom`/`maxzoom` | Look at the source config |
| `source-layer` names exactly match the `vector_layers.id` values in the TileJSON | Open the TileJSON and copy the IDs |
| Layers are added before the first `symbol` layer (so labels stay visible) | Use `addLayer(layer, firstSymbolId)` |
| Glyphs and sprite URLs are present (only needed for text/icons) | Usually inherited from the provider’s style; copy them if you build a style from scratch |

Once the source-layer names line up and the layers are inserted in the proper order, your custom style will render on top of the same tile source that the hosted style uses.

### Why the labels disappear  

MapLibre draws layers **bottom-to-top** in the order they appear in the style.  
When you call `map.addLayer()` without a second argument the new layer is appended
to the end of the list, i.e. **above everything else** – including all the
`symbol` layers that render street names, place labels, icons, etc.  
That’s why your GeoJSON layer is covering the labels.

### Fix: insert the layer *below* the first label layer  

Add the layer **before** the first `symbol` layer (or any other layer that you
want to stay on top). There are two common ways to do that:

#### 1. Pass the `beforeId` argument to `addLayer`

```js
// Find the first symbol layer in the current style
function getFirstSymbolLayerId() {
  const layers = map.getStyle().layers;
  for (const l of layers) {
    if (l.type === 'symbol') {
      return l.id;          // this is the first label layer
    }
  }
  return null; // fallback – no symbol layer found
}

const beforeId = getFirstSymbolLayerId();

map.addLayer(
  {
    id: 'my-geojson',
    type: 'fill',               // or line, circle, etc.
    source: 'my-geojson-source',
    paint: {
      'fill-color': '#ff0000',
      'fill-opacity': 0.4
    }
  },
  beforeId                     // ← insert below the first label layer
);
```

If `beforeId` is `null` (no symbol layer in the style), the layer will be added
at the top – which is fine because there are no labels to hide.

#### 2. Add the layer first, then move it later

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'my-geojson',
  type: 'fill',
  source: 'my-geojson-source',
  paint: { 'fill-color': '#ff0000', 'fill-opacity': 0.4 }
});

// After the style is loaded, move it below the first symbol layer
map.once('styledata', () => {
  const beforeId = getFirstSymbolLayerId();
  if (beforeId) {
    map.moveLayer('my-geojson', beforeId);
  }
});
```

### Quick checklist

| Step | What to do |
|------|------------|
| **Identify label layer** | Look for the first layer whose `type` is `"symbol"` (street names, place labels, icons). |
| **Add with `beforeId`** | Pass that layer’s `id` as the second argument to `map.addLayer()`. |
| **Or move later** | Call `map.moveLayer('your-layer-id', beforeId)` after the style is loaded. |
| **Verify** | The map should now show your GeoJSON **under** the street-name labels. |

That’s all you need – no other style changes are required. Your data will stay
visible, and the built-in labels will remain on top. Happy mapping!

You’re almost there – the source definition itself is fine, but a classic OSM raster tile service uses **256 px tiles**, while MapLibre’s raster source defaults to **512 px**. If you leave the default, the imagery will appear at the wrong zoom level (it looks “zoomed out” and can look blank at higher zooms).

Add the `tileSize` property (and, optionally, the usual metadata) and then add a raster layer that points at the source:

```js
// 1️⃣ Define the raster source
map.addSource('osm-basemap', {
  type: 'raster',
  tiles: ['https://tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'],
  tileSize: 256,          // <-- required for OSM tiles
  minzoom: 0,
  maxzoom: 19,
  attribution: '© OpenStreetMap contributors'
});

// 2️⃣ Add a layer that draws the source
map.addLayer({
  id: 'osm-basemap-layer',
  type: 'raster',
  source: 'osm-basemap',
  // optional: insert below the first symbol layer so you don’t cover labels
  // (replace 'road-label' with whatever the first symbol layer ID is in your style)
  // before: 'road-label'
});
```

**What this does**

| Item | Why it matters |
|------|----------------|
| `tileSize: 256` | OSM tiles are 256 px; without it the map renders at the wrong effective zoom. |
| `minzoom` / `maxzoom` | Gives MapLibre the correct zoom range (OSM serves 0-19). |
| `attribution` | Required by OSM’s usage policy and shows up in the map’s attribution control. |
| Layer insertion order | If you add the layer after existing symbol layers, it will hide street-name labels. Pass the ID of the first symbol layer as the second argument to `addLayer` (or use `before: 'road-label'` as shown) to keep labels on top. |

OSM’s tile server already sends the proper CORS headers, so no extra CORS configuration is needed. With the `tileSize` set to 256 (and the layer added), the tiles will render correctly.

No. A MapLibre **style** (the JSON that contains `version`, `sources`, `layers`, `sprite`, `glyphs`, etc.) is not a source that you can add with `map.addSource()`.  
A source is a single data provider (e.g., a vector tile set, a raster tile set, a GeoJSON file). The style JSON is a *collection* of sources plus the layers that draw from them, plus the sprite and glyph definitions.

To reuse just one background layer from that style you need to:

1. **Add the source that the background layer uses**  
   ```js
   // copy the source definition from the external style
   map.addSource('external-basemap', {
     type: 'vector',          // or 'raster' etc.
     url: 'https://example.com/tiles.json'   // prefer the TileJSON `url`
     // if only a tiles template is available, use `tiles: [...]` and set minzoom/maxzoom
   });
   ```

2. **Add the layer that draws the background**  
   ```js
   map.addLayer({
     id: 'external-background',
     type: 'background',          // or 'fill', 'raster', etc. – whatever the original layer type is
     source: 'external-basemap',
     'source-layer': 'background', // exact name from the source’s tile schema
     paint: { /* copy the paint properties from the original layer */ }
   });
   ```

3. **Make sure the `source-layer` name matches the tile schema**  
   Look at the TileJSON’s `vector_layers` (or the provider’s documentation) to get the correct `source-layer` name. A typo or a name from a different schema will cause the layer to draw nothing, with no error.

4. **Handle sprite and glyph assets**  
   The external style’s `sprite` and `glyphs` URLs are **style-level** properties, not source properties. If the background layer uses icons or text, you must also set those URLs on your map’s style (or copy the needed icons into your own sprite). Example:

   ```js
   map.setStyle({
     ...yourExistingStyle,
     sprite: 'https://example.com/sprite',
     glyphs: 'https://example.com/fonts/{fontstack}/{range}.pbf'
   });
   ```

   If you already have a style loaded, you can update them with `map.setSprite()` / `map.setGlyphs()` (or simply reload the style with the combined configuration).

5. **Insert the layer at the right order**  
   Adding the layer with `map.addLayer()` without a second argument puts it on top of everything, which may hide labels. To keep the basemap’s labels visible, insert the new background **below** the first `symbol` layer:

   ```js
   const firstSymbol = map.getStyle().layers.find(l => l.type === 'symbol').id;
   map.addLayer(backgroundLayer, firstSymbol);
   ```

In short: you cannot treat the whole style JSON as a source. Extract the needed source definition, add that source, then add the specific background layer (copying its `type`, `source-layer`, `paint`, etc.) and make sure sprite/glyph URLs are available. This wires the external background correctly on top of your own basemap.

**What’s happening**

`setFeatureState` attaches a piece of state to a *feature id* that lives inside the source.  
Your parcels have an identifier **inside `properties.parcel_id`**, but the source does not know that this property should be used as the feature’s id.  
When you call

```js
map.setFeatureState(
  { source: 'parcels', id: '12345' },   // <-- id you think exists
  { hover: true }
);
```

MapLibre looks for a feature whose **top-level `id`** equals `12345`.  
Because the source has no such id, the call succeeds silently, the state is never stored, and the paint expression that reads `["feature-state", "hover"]` never sees anything.

The fix is to tell the source to **promote** the `parcel_id` property to the feature id (or, alternatively, add a top-level `id` yourself). Once the source knows the id, `setFeatureState` works and the expression will react.

---

## Step-by-step wiring

### 1. Add the GeoJSON source with `promoteId`

```js
map.addSource('parcels', {
  type: 'geojson',
  data: 'https://example.com/parcels.geojson',   // or a raw GeoJSON object
  promoteId: 'parcel_id'                         // <-- the key that becomes the feature id
});
```

* `promoteId` can be a string (applies to every source-layer) or an object `{ <sourceLayer>: <property> }`.  
  For a plain GeoJSON source there is only one “layer”, so the string form is enough.

### 2. Add a layer that uses the source-layer and reads the state

```js
map.addLayer({
  id: 'parcel-fill',
  type: 'fill',
  source: 'parcels',
  // no source-layer needed for GeoJSON sources
  paint: {
    // default fill
    'fill-color': '#88c0d0',
    'fill-opacity': 0.4,

    // hover colour – only applied when the feature-state key `hover` is true
    'fill-color': [
      'case',
      ['boolean', ['feature-state', 'hover'], false],
      '#ffcc00',               // colour when hovered
      '#88c0d0'                // normal colour
    ]
  }
});
```

> **Why the `case` expression?**  
> The `feature-state` expression returns `undefined` when no state exists, which is falsy. Wrapping it in `boolean` guarantees a true/false value for the `case`.

### 3. Wire the mouse events

```js
let hoveredId = null;

map.on('mousemove', 'parcel-fill', (e) => {
  if (!e.features.length) return;

  const feature = e.features[0];
  const id = feature.id;               // <-- this is now the promoted parcel_id

  if (hoveredId !== null && hoveredId !== id) {
    // clear previous hover
    map.setFeatureState({ source: 'parcels', id: hoveredId }, { hover: false });
  }

  hoveredId = id;
  map.setFeatureState({ source: 'parcels', id }, { hover: true });
});

map.on('mouseleave', 'parcel-fill', () => {
  if (hoveredId !== null) {
    map.setFeatureState({ source: 'parcels', id: hoveredId }, { hover: false });
    hoveredId = null;
  }
});
```

* **Important:** The `id` you pass to `setFeatureState` must be exactly the value that MapLibre promoted. Using `feature.id` (as shown) guarantees you’re using the right one.

### 4. (Optional) Verify the state works

```js
// In the console, after hovering a parcel:
map.getFeatureState({ source: 'parcels', id: hoveredId });
// → { hover: true }
```

If you get an empty object, the source still isn’t promoting the id correctly—double-check the property name and that the source was added **before** any `setFeatureState` calls.

---

## Common pitfalls that cause the same symptom

| Symptom | Typical cause | Fix |
|---------|---------------|-----|
| `setFeatureState` does nothing, no console error | No `id` on the feature (missing `promoteId` or `id` field) | Add `promoteId` (or top-level `id`) |
| Hover works on the first feature but then stops | Using `generateId: true` on a source that already has a business key | Use `promoteId` instead; `generateId` overwrites existing ids and is unstable across data updates |
| State appears only after a full `setData` call | Source was added *after* you called `setFeatureState` (the source didn’t exist yet) | Call `setFeatureState` **after** the source is added and the data is loaded (`map.on('load')` or after `source.setData`) |
| Paint expression never fires | Using `["feature-state", "hover"]` directly without a boolean guard (returns `undefined`) | Wrap in `["boolean", …]` or use a `case` as shown above |
| Hover works but disappears when you pan/zoom | The source is re-loaded (e.g., `source.setData` on every move) which clears feature state | Keep the source static, or re-apply the state after each `setData` if you must replace the data |

---

## Minimal reproducible example (copy-paste)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>Parcel hover with feature-state</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',
    center: [-122.45, 37.78],
    zoom: 12
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ Add the GeoJSON source with promoteId
    map.addSource('parcels', {
      type: 'geojson',
      data: 'https://raw.githubusercontent.com/public-data/geojson-samples/main/parcels.geojson',
      promoteId: 'parcel_id'
    });

    // 2️⃣ Add a fill layer that reacts to feature-state
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'parcel-fill',
      type: 'fill',
      source: 'parcels',
      paint: {
        'fill-color': [
          'case',
          ['boolean', ['feature-state', 'hover'], false],
          '#ffcc00',
          '#88c0d0'
        ],
        'fill-opacity': 0.5
      }
    });

    // 3️⃣ Wire hover events
    let hoveredId = null;
    map.on('mousemove', 'parcel-fill', (e) => {
      if (!e.features.length) return;
      const id = e.features[0].id;
      if (hoveredId !== null && hoveredId !== id) {
        map.setFeatureState({ source: 'parcels', id: hoveredId }, { hover: false });
      }
      hoveredId = id;
      map.setFeatureState({ source: 'parcels', id }, { hover: true });
    });

    map.on('mouseleave', 'parcel-fill', () => {
      if (hoveredId !== null) {
        map.setFeatureState({ source: 'parcels', id: hoveredId }, { hover: false });
        hoveredId = null;
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Run the page, move the mouse over a parcel polygon, and it should turn yellow. Open the console and type `map.getFeatureState({source:'parcels', id: <the hovered parcel_id>})` to see the stored state.

---

### TL;DR checklist

1. **Give the source an id** – `promoteId: 'parcel_id'` (or add a top-level `id`).  
2. Use that id (`feature.id`) when calling `setFeatureState`.  
3. Make the paint expression read the state (`["boolean", ["feature-state","hover"], false]`).  
4. Call `setFeatureState` only after the source is added and the data is loaded.  

Once those four pieces line up, hover highlighting works reliably. Happy mapping!
